#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bff423b-022b-5914-a134-c4c346e91b8f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "70e3ea0e-2f39-5715-98a3-2a0e686ffa76" :question/math-academy-id "e-10116" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta)$ for $\\theta \\in \\left[0, 2\\pi\\right)$. Given that\n\n$x'(\\theta) = 2\\cos2\\theta - 2\\sin\\theta, \\qquad y'(\\theta) = 2\\cos\\theta+2\\sin2\\theta$,\nand $x'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right) = 0$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi/2} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=0$\n3. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point $\\theta_0$, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative $y'(x)$ takes the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nat this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that\n\n$$\nx'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right)=0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}2$ into the expression for $y'(\\theta)$, we get\n$y^{′}(\\frac{3π}{2})|= 2\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}); = 2\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\sin (3π); = 0 + 0; = 0$.\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n$lim_(θ → 3π/2)\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → 3π/2)(y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(\\frac{2\\cos θ + 2\\sin 2θ}{2\\cos 2θ - 2\\sin θ}); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(\\frac{\\cos θ + \\sin 2θ}{\\cos 2θ - \\sin θ}); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(((\\cos θ + \\sin 2θ)^{′})/((\\cos 2θ - \\sin θ)^{′})); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(\\frac{-\\sin θ + 2\\cos 2θ}{-2\\sin 2θ - \\cos θ}); = (-\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}))/(-2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}) - \\cos (\\frac{3π}{2})); = (-\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\cos (3π))/(-2\\sin (3π) - \\cos (\\frac{3π}{2})); = (1 + 2 \\cdot (-1))/(-2 \\cdot 0 - 0); = \\frac{1 - 2}{0}; = \\frac{-1}{0}; \\ne 0$.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0$, there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$. In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/1b/1bff05165761ba775dfae73cd4d41c28f5a939b8caca602d516636a6fb05ae75.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note**: We'll discuss vertical tangents in more detail later in this lesson."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188023 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25fd0bb9-4a47-5f76-bdba-cdcd19cf141f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "2175ea86-60e7-5402-810d-1372b455c28d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10244" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\cot^2\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in \\left(0, \\pi\\right)$. Given that\n\n$x'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta(\\cos\\theta + 2\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta), \\qquad y'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta(1+\\sin^2\\theta)$,\nand $y'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right) = 0$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=-\\infty$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n$\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ → 0^{+}asθ → (\\frac{π}{2})^{-}; \\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ → 0^{-}asθ → (\\frac{π}{2})^{+}$\n\n*It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\theta_0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) = 0\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nat this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that\n\n$$\ny'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)=0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi2$ into the expression for $x'(\\theta)$, we get\n$x^{′}(\\frac{π}{2})|=-\\cot (\\frac{π}{2})(\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + 2\\cot (\\frac{π}{2})\\csc (\\frac{π}{2})); = 0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 1); = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^+$, we obtain\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})((-\\cot θ\\csc θ(1 + \\sin^{2} θ))/(-\\cot θ(\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ))); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})((-\\cotθ\\csc θ(1 + \\sin^{2} θ))/(-\\cotθ(\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ))); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})((\\csc θ(1 + \\sin^{2} θ))/(\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ)); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(\\frac{\\csc θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ})$.\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(\\csc θ + \\sin θ)|= \\csc (\\frac{π}{2}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}); = 1 + 1; = 2$.\nTherefore, using the second hint, we have\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(\\frac{\\csc θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ}); = \\frac{2}{0^{-}}; = - ∞$.\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part I, and the first hint, we have\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})(\\frac{\\csc θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ}); = \\frac{2}{0^{+}}; = ∞$.\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2dc0665a0f979fd247d010a7b6d5891a758eff7e5357b881011ddf0d3f2f2002.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bff423b-022b-5914-a134-c4c346e91b8f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1a30c16-211c-5885-bd73-60eba0b26337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185677} {:answer/id #uuid "eebe674e-a895-53a5-8284-bb2fe739487c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185678} {:answer/id #uuid "00554a1e-d43f-5aca-89a9-07841ea6af07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185679} {:answer/id #uuid "3472b7ea-9bc6-58cd-bf1a-0790d8d48e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185680} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8d629-42cc-5562-a4b8-39f1b9893e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185681}] :answer-field/id #uuid "320ef1c8-e7ec-58a4-b0b7-68dce67c7b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185676}] :question/id #uuid "46af20ff-a82f-5003-946a-59bcece07f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143555" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r = 1 - \\sin θ$ for $θ ∈ [0, 2π)$. Given that\n\n$x^{′}(θ) =-\\sin (θ) - \\cos (2θ), y^{′}(θ) = \\cos (θ) - \\sin (2θ)$,\nand $x^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) = 0$\n2. $lim_(θ → π/2)\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$\n3. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $θ = π/2$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25fd0bb9-4a47-5f76-bdba-cdcd19cf141f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6cd7e58-adc6-5d97-83dc-0ef80f38597f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185683} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe0e570-a22a-5641-93a8-f6671eba3fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185684} {:answer/id #uuid "2b2627b4-92aa-5fc7-b855-d9ad614c65ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185685} {:answer/id #uuid "37a2400b-425f-50cd-9cf2-4636a9355424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185686} {:answer/id #uuid "61adcb0d-059f-5544-a8ef-924174a4c953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185687}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a6707d96-ec88-5b35-acfc-f90c52c72973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185682}] :question/id #uuid "9cda14ea-b780-59bc-8a48-d618ff7d920f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143587" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r = \\sin θ - 1$ for $θ ∈ [0, 2π)$. Given that\n\n$x^{′}(θ) = \\sin (θ) + \\cos (2θ), y^{′}(θ) =-\\cos (θ) + \\sin (2θ)$,\nand $x^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) = 0$\n2. $lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx} = ∞$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $θ = \\frac{π}{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n$\\cos (θ) - 2\\sin (2θ) → 0^{-}asθ → (\\frac{π}{2})^{-}; \\cos (θ) - 2\\sin (2θ) → 0^{+}asθ → (\\frac{π}{2})^{+}$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
